F) function        field three
G) params          field four
K) output          how steps are reported

.SH A) OVERVIEW
code-steps are the inserting of pure c-language code into the final unit-test.
no matter how useful other verbs are, they are to cover the 80%.  code lines
fill in the specific, unusual, irregular, and rare.

.SH B) TECHNICAL
exec-steps are translated function calls, result evaluation, and statistic
capture within a condition and script.

.SH C) EXAMPLE
code-steps have three fields.  the leading spaces are changed to spacers (·)
in this manual for clarity.

   verb------ § --desc-- § func § ---------------code-------------------- §
   ·····code  § chg dir  § - -  § chdir (u_dir_gyges);                    §
   ·····code  § string   § - -  § sprintf (x_header, "%s.h", u_gyges);    §
   ·····code  § save     § - -  § x_save = x_new;  x_new = NULL;          §
   ·····code  § init     § - -  § s_1st->c_type = '!';                    §

these four examples are common...
   1) changing directories to a temporary testing area
   2) creating a working string for a file name
   3) saving off a value for later testing
   4) re-initializing a flag to start another condition

NOTE: include, global and local variables could all be done with CODE, but
i wanted those other things to stand out more clearly.

.SH D) VERB
the verb CODE is expected to be indented five (5) characters off-estting it
from SCRP and later steps.  the verb field are is twelve (12) characters,
overflow and trailing spaces will be truncated.

   verb------ § --desc-- § func § ---------------code-------------------- §
   ·····code  § ·        § ·    § ·                                       §
   ·····code  § ·        § ·    § ·                                       §
   ·····code  § ·        § ·    § ·                                       §
   ·····code  § ·        § ·    § ·                                       §

.SH E) DESCRIPTION
it is very unhelpful to leave off a description as unit-testing is almost
invariably detailed, complicated, and extensive.  descriptions are a maximum
of thirty-five (35) characters, overflow and trailing spaces will be truncated.
legal characters are letters, numbers, and most punctuation.

since descriptions are demonstrated elsewhere, i have truncated them greatly
to fit everything in the available space ;)

   verb------ § --desc-- § func § ---------------code-------------------- §
   ·····code  § chg dir  § ·    § ·                                       §
   ·····code  § string   § ·    § ·                                       §
   ·····code  § save     § ·    § ·                                       §
   ·····code  § init     § ·    § ·                                       §

.SH F) FUNCTION
the third field is left blank.

   verb------ § --desc-- § func § ---------------code-------------------- §
   ·····code  § ·        § - -  § ·                                       §
   ·····code  § ·        § - -  § ·                                       §
   ·····code  § ·        § - -  § ·                                       §
   ·····code  § ·        § - -  § ·                                       §

.SH G) PARAMS
the fourth field is the actual c_language code to be executed.  it must be
pure 'c' as it will not be modified before placing in unit-test.

   verb------ § --desc-- § func § ---------------code-------------------- §
   ·····code  § ·        § ·    § chdir (u_dir_gyges);                    §
   ·····code  § ·        § ·    § sprintf (x_header, "%s.h", u_gyges);    §
   ·····code  § ·        § ·    § x_save = x_new;  x_new = NULL;          §
   ·····code  § ·        § ·    § s_1st->c_type = '!';                    §

.SH H) OUTPUT
when the unit-test is run, each code-step generates small block of output.
the output says it is the 2nd step in the condition (ab) and is generated by
line 38985 in the source code.  everything else is a reflection of input values.
variables are never pass/fail in statistics.

  ···ab) CODE   : chg dir ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·º38985»
  ·······code   :  29åchdir (u_dir_gyges);æ

NOTE: if the line number is [00555] is is in a specific unit test source file.
if like å00555æ it is in a shared file -- unit_data.unit or unit_wide.unit.

